        'nav_label' => 'HVAC Installation',
        'group' => 'cooling',
        'icon' => 'wind',
        'image' => 'technicians-condenser-installation.jpg',
        'featured' => true,
        'short' => 'Whole-home heating and cooling installation for new systems, additions, and homes upgrading to central air, furnaces, heat pumps, or ductless units.',
        'hero_heading' => 'Professional HVAC installation options for your home',
        'hero_text' => 'Planning a new heating or cooling system for your home? HVAC Services is an independent referral service that helps homeowners connect with available local HVAC providers for residential system sizing and installation.',
        'seo_title' => 'Residential HVAC Installation Services | HVAC Services',
        'seo_description' => 'Explore residential HVAC installation options for your home. Connect with independent local HVAC service providers for central air, furnaces, heat pumps, and ductless systems.',
        'overview' => '<p>Installing a complete heating and air conditioning system is one of the most important improvements you can make to your home\'s comfort and day-to-day energy use. Whether you are adding central comfort to an older house without ductwork, outfitting a newly finished addition, or installing matched indoor and outdoor equipment for the first time, the quality of the design and installation matters just as much as the brand on the equipment cabinet.</p><p>HVAC Services is an independent referral service for U.S. homeowners. When you call or submit a request, we work to connect you with an independent local HVAC provider who can inspect your home, perform a room-by-room load calculation, explain suitable equipment configurations, and provide a written installation quote.</p>',
        'problems' => [
            ['title' => 'Load calculation and sizing', 'text' => 'A Manual J calculation accounts for square footage, window orientation, insulation levels, air leakage, and regional climate rather than guessing by floor area alone.'],
            ['title' => 'Ductwork design and airflow', 'text' => 'Supply and return ducts must be sized and sealed to deliver balanced airflow to each room without excessive static pressure or noise.'],
            ['title' => 'Electrical, gas, and venting readiness', 'text' => 'New systems often require dedicated breaker capacity, properly sized gas piping, or PVC flue venting and condensate drainage for condensing equipment.'],
            ['title' => 'Thermostat and zoning placement', 'text' => 'Placing sensors away from direct sunlight, drafts, and supply registers helps multi-stage and zoned systems maintain steady temperatures across floors.'],
        ],
        'options' => [
            ['title' => 'Split central AC and furnace systems', 'text' => 'Pairs an outdoor air conditioning condenser with an indoor gas or electric furnace and evaporator coil using whole-home ductwork.'],
            ['title' => 'All-electric heat pump systems', 'text' => 'Uses a single outdoor heat pump and indoor air handler to provide both summer cooling and winter heating through ducts.'],
            ['title' => 'Ductless mini-split installations', 'text' => 'Connects an outdoor unit to one or more room-mounted air handlers without tearing into walls to build new ductwork.'],
            ['title' => 'Dual-fuel and zoned systems', 'text' => 'Combines a heat pump with a backup furnace or motorized duct dampers for multi-story homes with distinct comfort zones.'],
        ],
        'signs' => [
            'Your home currently relies on window air conditioners, space heaters, or aging baseboard units',
            'You are building a home addition, finishing an attic or basement, or remodeling living space',
            'Upper and lower floors stay several degrees apart during summer heat or winter cold',
            'Existing ductwork or equipment was never sized for the current layout of the house',
            'You want to compare central ducted systems against ductless or heat pump alternatives',
        ],
        'details' => '<h2>Energy efficiency and home comfort</h2><p>Modern residential HVAC systems are rated using <strong>SEER2</strong> for cooling, <strong>HSPF2</strong> for heat pump heating, and <strong>AFUE</strong> for fuel-burning furnaces. Beyond efficiency numbers, comfort depends on how the equipment runs: two-stage and variable-speed systems can operate at lower output for longer periods, which removes more indoor humidity in summer and avoids sharp temperature swings in winter.</p><h2>What homeowners should discuss with a service provider</h2><ul><li>Did the provider perform a heat-gain and heat-loss calculation for the home before recommending tonnage or BTU capacity?</li><li>Are the indoor coil, air handler or furnace, and outdoor condenser listed as a matched combination?</li><li>What modifications to return air ducts, filter cabinets, refrigerant line sets, or condensate drains are included in the scope of work?</li><li>Who pulls the local mechanical or electrical permits and schedules municipal inspections where required?</li><li>What are the terms of the manufacturer equipment warranty and the independent contractor\'s labor warranty?</li></ul><p>Looking to replace an existing worn-out unit instead of starting from scratch? Explore our guide to <a href="/services/hvac-replacement">residential HVAC replacement</a> or learn about <a href="/services/heat-pump">heat pump systems</a> and <a href="/services/ductless">ductless mini-splits</a>.</p>',
        'faqs' => [
            ['question' => 'How do I know what size HVAC system my home needs?', 'answer' => 'An independent HVAC contractor should perform a Manual J load calculation that evaluates your home\'s square footage, ceiling height, insulation, window types, sun exposure, and local climate zone. Sizing by square footage alone often leads to oversized equipment that short cycles and leaves the air humid.'],
            ['question' => 'How long does a residential HVAC installation usually take?', 'answer' => 'Timelines depend on the scope of work. A straightforward equipment installation using existing ducts is often completed in one to two days, while projects involving new ductwork, zoning, electrical panel upgrades, or multi-zone mini-splits can take several days. Ask your provider for a project schedule with your quote.'],
            ['question' => 'Do I need local permits for a new HVAC installation?', 'answer' => 'Most cities and counties require mechanical permits—and sometimes electrical or gas permits—for installing new heating and cooling equipment. Always ask the independent contractor you hire to confirm permit requirements and inspection procedures in writing.'],
            ['question' => 'Does HVAC Services install the equipment directly?', 'answer' => 'No. HVAC Services is an independent referral service that connects homeowners with participating local HVAC service providers. All assessments, quotes, permits, and installation work are performed by the independent contractor you choose to hire.'],
        ],
        'related' => ['hvac-replacement', 'heat-pump', 'ductless'],
