    [
        'slug' => 'hvac-replacement',
        'title' => 'Residential HVAC Replacement Services',
        'nav_label' => 'HVAC Replacement',
        'group' => 'cooling',
        'icon' => 'refresh',
        'image' => 'technician-reviewing-options-with-customer.jpg',
        'featured' => true,
        'short' => 'Guidance and local provider connections for homeowners deciding whether to repair or replace an aging air conditioner, furnace, or heat pump.',
        'hero_heading' => 'Ready to replace your home\'s HVAC system?',
        'hero_text' => 'When an older heating or cooling system needs frequent repairs or struggles to keep up, HVAC Services helps you connect with independent local providers to compare replacement options.',
        'seo_title' => 'Residential HVAC Replacement Services | HVAC Services',
        'seo_description' => 'Planning to replace an aging heating or cooling system? Learn how to compare repair versus replacement options and connect with independent local HVAC providers.',
        'overview' => '<p>Every residential heating and cooling system eventually reaches a point where another repair is no longer the most practical investment. Air conditioners and heat pumps commonly serve a home for 10 to 15 years, while gas and electric furnaces often last 15 to 20 years depending on climate, run hours, and maintenance history.</p><p>Replacing a system gives you an opportunity to solve comfort complaints that may have existed for years—such as noisy airflow, humid summer rooms, or uneven winter heat—while moving to current refrigerants and efficiency standards. HVAC Services is an independent referral service that connects homeowners with participating local contractors who can inspect your current setup and provide transparent replacement quotes.</p>',
        'problems' => [
            ['title' => 'Repair cost vs. remaining equipment life', 'text' => 'When a compressor, heat exchanger, or indoor coil fails on an out-of-warranty unit, the repair bill can represent a large fraction of a new system.'],
            ['title' => 'Phased-out refrigerants', 'text' => 'Older cooling systems that rely on R-22 refrigerant face steep recharge costs when a coil or line set develops a leak.'],
            ['title' => 'Mismatched indoor and outdoor units', 'text' => 'Replacing only one half of an older split system can reduce efficiency and cause premature wear if components are not properly matched.'],
            ['title' => 'Changes to the home since original install', 'text' => 'Upgraded windows, added attic insulation, or remodeled rooms can change your home\'s heating and cooling load, making a fresh load calculation worthwhile.'],
        ],
        'options' => [
            ['title' => 'Complete furnace and AC replacement', 'text' => 'Replacing both the furnace and central air conditioner together ensures matched airflow, shared controls, and a single installation visit.'],
            ['title' => 'Matched condenser and evaporator coil swap', 'text' => 'When the furnace is relatively new, an installer can replace the outdoor AC condenser and indoor coil as a compatible cooling pair.'],
            ['title' => 'Switching from AC to a heat pump', 'text' => 'Many homeowners replacing a central AC choose a heat pump so the outdoor unit can also handle mild-to-cold-weather heating.'],
            ['title' => 'Two-stage and variable-speed upgrades', 'text' => 'Modulating compressors and variable-speed blowers run quieter and hold indoor temperatures and humidity closer to the thermostat setting.'],
        ],
        'signs' => [
            'Your air conditioner or heat pump is more than 12 years old, or your furnace is past 15 years',
            'You have needed multiple service calls over the last two heating or cooling seasons',
            'A technician has diagnosed a cracked heat exchanger, failed compressor, or major coil leak',
            'Monthly utility bills are climbing even though thermostat settings have not changed',
            'The system runs almost continuously on hot afternoons or cold nights without reaching set temperature',
        ],
        'details' => '<h2>Repair versus replacement considerations</h2><p>A single failed capacitor, contactor, igniter, or sensor on a mid-life system is usually straightforward to repair. Replacement becomes the stronger option when major sealed components fail after the manufacturer warranty expires, when safety components like a furnace heat exchanger are compromised, or when cumulative repair costs keep mounting each season.</p><h2>Energy efficiency considerations</h2><p>Federal efficiency standards updated to <strong>SEER2</strong> and <strong>HSPF2</strong> testing procedures mean that today\'s entry-level and mid-tier systems often use noticeably less energy than equipment installed 15 years ago. Upgrading the filter cabinet and sealing accessible duct leaks during the replacement can also improve airflow and indoor air quality.</p><h2>Questions to ask a contractor before replacing</h2><ul><li>Can you show me a repair estimate alongside one or two replacement options so I can compare them side by side?</li><li>Will the refrigerant line set, disconnect box, whip, pad, and condensate safety switches be replaced or reused?</li><li>Does my existing ductwork have enough return air capacity for the new blower?</li><li>Are any utility, state, or manufacturer rebates currently available for the models quoted?</li><li>What is the homeowner\'s responsibility for registering equipment warranties after installation?</li></ul><p>You can also compare <a href="/services/hvac-installation">new HVAC installation options</a>, <a href="/services/heating">residential heating services</a>, and <a href="/services/heat-pump">heat pump systems</a> before speaking with a local provider.</p>',
        'faqs' => [
            ['question' => 'Should I replace my furnace and air conditioner at the same time?', 'answer' => 'If both units are more than 12–15 years old, replacing them together often saves on installation labor and ensures that the indoor blower, coil, and outdoor unit are engineered to work as a matched system. If your furnace is only a few years old, replacing just the outdoor unit and indoor evaporator coil may be practical.'],
            ['question' => 'Can my existing ductwork be reused when replacing an HVAC system?', 'answer' => 'Often yes, provided the ducts are in good condition and sized properly for the new equipment\'s airflow requirements. Ask the contractor to inspect the return air drop, plenums, and static pressure so hidden duct restrictions do not choke the new system.'],
            ['question' => 'What happens to my old HVAC equipment during a replacement?', 'answer' => 'Independent installers typically recover the existing refrigerant in accordance with EPA rules and haul away the old condenser, coil, and furnace for recycling or disposal. Confirm that equipment removal and disposal are listed on your written quote.'],
            ['question' => 'Does HVAC Services provide replacement quotes directly?', 'answer' => 'No. HVAC Services is a free referral service that connects homeowners with independent HVAC contractors. The provider you meet with inspects your home and supplies the estimate, contract, and warranty terms.'],
