        'details' => '<h2>Furnace safety and when to call for immediate help</h2><p>Any fuel-burning appliance requires proper venting and complete combustion. Keep working carbon monoxide alarms on every level of your home and outside sleeping areas. If you ever smell natural gas or propane, or if a carbon monoxide alarm sounds, evacuate the house immediately and call 911 or your gas utility from outside before arranging heating repair.</p><h2>Heating installation and replacement considerations</h2><p>When an older furnace faces a major repair—such as a cracked heat exchanger, failed control module, or corroded secondary heat exchanger—comparing repair costs against a new high-efficiency furnace or dual-fuel heat pump makes sense. A qualified contractor can evaluate your climate, fuel rates, flue chimney or PVC venting path, and electrical panel capacity to help you choose.</p><h2>Seasonal heating maintenance</h2><p>Having a technician inspect your heating system each autumn helps catch small wear items—such as a weak run capacitor or a coated flame sensor—before the first freezing night of winter. Learn more on our dedicated pages for <a href="/services/furnace-repair">furnace repair</a>, <a href="/services/heating-repair">heating repair</a>, and <a href="/services/hvac-maintenance">seasonal HVAC maintenance</a>.</p>',
