        'details' => '<h2>Heat pumps versus traditional furnace and AC combinations</h2><p>A traditional split system uses a central air conditioner for summer and a separate furnace for winter. A heat pump replaces the outdoor AC condenser with a reversible unit that handles both jobs. In mild and moderate climates, an all-electric heat pump with an air handler can serve the entire home year-round. In colder northern states, homeowners often choose either a cold-climate inverter heat pump or a dual-fuel system that pairs a heat pump with a gas furnace.</p><h2>Climate and home suitability considerations</h2><p>When comparing heat pump models, look at both <strong>SEER2</strong> (cooling efficiency) and <strong>HSPF2</strong> (heating efficiency), as well as the manufacturer\'s low-temperature heating capacity ratings at 47°F and 5°F. Proper duct sizing and weatherization also play an important role because heat pumps deliver a steady stream of warm air rather than the high-temperature blasts of a combustion furnace.</p><h2>Choosing a service provider</h2><p>Because heat pumps operate in both summer and winter, accurate refrigerant charge—often weighed in and verified by superheat and subcooling measurements—and proper thermostat staging are essential. When you speak with an independent contractor, ask about their experience configuring balance points for auxiliary heat and whether your home needs any electrical or ductwork updates. See also our guides to <a href="/services/ductless">ductless mini-split heat pumps</a> and <a href="/services/hvac-replacement">HVAC system replacement</a>.</p>',
        'faqs' => [
