        'details' => '<h2>Ductless mini-splits versus central air conditioning</h2><p>Central HVAC systems condition the entire house from a single indoor air handler and distribute air through supply and return ducts—which works well in homes built with properly sized, well-sealed ductwork. However, in older homes without ducts, retrofitting trunk lines often requires sacrificing closet space or building soffits. Ductless mini-splits require only a roughly three-inch opening through the exterior wall for the line set, eliminate duct energy losses, and provide built-in room-by-room zoning.</p><h2>Ductless installation considerations</h2><ul><li><strong>Room-by-room sizing:</strong> Each indoor head must be matched to the heat gain and heat loss of its specific room; oversizing a mini-split in a small bedroom can cause short cycling and poor humidity removal.</li><li><strong>Indoor unit placement:</strong> Wall heads need clear airflow away from tall furniture and a practical path to route condensate drainage by gravity or via a quiet condensate pump.</li><li><strong>Outdoor unit mounting:</strong> In regions with winter snowfall, the outdoor heat pump should be mounted on a stand or wall bracket above typical snow accumulation so defrost meltwater can drain freely.</li></ul><p>Compare ductless options with <a href="/services/heat-pump">central heat pump services</a>, <a href="/services/hvac-installation">residential HVAC installation</a>, and <a href="/services/indoor-air-quality">indoor air quality solutions</a>.</p>',
